<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inshushinak</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Inshushinak was the patron god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, whose name means 'lord of Susa' in Sumerian. A god of oaths and of the dead, he became from the 2nd millennium BCE one of the chief gods of the Elamite pantheon, honoured in royal names such as Puzur-Inshushinak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shilhak-inshushinak">Shilhak-Inshushinak</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ziggurat">ziggurat</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chogha-zanbil">Chogha Zanbil</a> was dedicated to him and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napirisha">Napirisha</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inshushinak.jpg" alt="Inshushinak" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rama · CC BY-SA 3.0 fr · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:God_golden_hand-Sb_2823-IMG_0815-gradient.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
